uh let's uh now speak to christopher  wilson he's a royal biographer and  commentator he joins me now from devon  good to have you on the program  your reaction to uh what we're hearing  at the moment  well of course i think we just have to  wait and see how things go but uh  i've been speaking to people in scotland  and i think that we can  take it that the queen's life is coming  to an end  um  tell us a little bit about uh  how she's been doing recently i mean  we've been hearing about  uh how she's had some mobility issues  which is why she's been uh based in  balmoral rather than sort of traveling  to the traveling to london which she  normally would have  what are you hearing about that  well i i i think what is remarkable  about this uh sovereign is that she has  clung to  uh the throne and to her job until the  very last moment i think we can see that  her powers have been declining for the  last year at the very least  and she has done a very very good job in  managing uh  what she's able to do  and putting from her things that she  wasn't able to do she's gone on far  longer than anybody could expect her to  do she's 96 years old and she's doing uh  which she has done all that she possibly  can to retain the strings of power and  the very idea that she was able to stand  upright a couple of days ago and greet  the new prime minister it would have  been an  iron will and determination on her part  that she should do that to show that she  is still sovereign until the moment she  dies  indeed a very beautiful heartwarming  picture uh and liz truss will have been  glad to meet her on that day  i mean she she she's such an important  person isn't she for the people of the  united kingdom and people all over  why  give us a sense i mean your royal  biographer why does she mean so much to  so many  i think that uh  she is a thread of continuity which has  taken the nation through i mean there  are people who are 80 or 90 years old in  great britain uh who  as it were saw this princess become a  queen and have lived with her uh through  thick and thin through changing times  all that time it's very reassuring in in  a changing world that some things remain  the same  and then for each new generation that's  come people have been born and died  within the space of her reign she has  always been there she is a constant in  people's lives in this country she has  meant so much  let's talk a little bit about her  husband prince philip who  had been by her side  from a very very long time from when she  was in her early 20s  he's been he he was with her throughout  the entire time he stood by her side how  difficult  do you think it's been for her since  losing him  i i think that we can see there was a  turn in the road at the moment that he  died at that point uh she weakened  perceptibly uh and perfectly  understandably i mean he had stood by  her he had been her support the only  person really in the whole royal family  that she could trust and rely on and uh  you know the loss of him at her  extremely  advanced age was really more uh than she  was able to cope with but and and so  what we saw was this slight retreat but  it was nothing like the retreat of other  uh sovereigns in other times she kept on  doing what she could do she kept on  being as visible as she could she  mastered  doing zoom meetings uh  and so forth in order to be able to keep  in contact with the outside world and to  show that she was still queen it was  quite a remarkable act but of course the  loss of prince philip really did change  the game yes she was absolutely  incredible always looked  fantastic at her best in the most  beautiful of clothes of suits uh and as  you say  even two days ago yeah uh she had this  astonishing smile there has been a  political turmoil in britain for the  last three or four months  and  her vision of  the politicians which serve this country  must possibly have deteriorated  and yet she greeted the new prime  minister with a wonderful welcoming  smile and a handshake she was even  carrying her signature handbag even  though  there was no need for that but you know  she was determined to show that the show  still goes on indeed it was such a  wonderful picture  britain of course going through a very  very difficult time the people of the  united kingdom we've been talking about  the the the high cost of living the  energy bills it's it's such a difficult  time for them  and the queen of course means so much to  so many i mean it couldn't this sort of  news couldn't come at a worse time could  it  i think that's absolutely right i mean  we have as you say we've gone through a  very difficult time recently uh and  rather like uh the the king and queen  during the second world war george vi  and the green mother  uh  if everything was really horrible what  you could do is rest your thoughts on  something neutral and mildly glamorous  and and  rest your mind and the queen provided  that in these times all the way through  covered all the way through uh the  problems which we've had to face ever  since she's been a constant something  that is non-controversial non-political  uh and uh just something that you can  rest your mind on and feel good about  uh you are a biographer uh and i i i  just want to ask you  the way things are being handled at the  moment the fact that we we received the  statement from buckingham palace  uh we know that prince charles  is there with her in balmoral uh and  prince william is on his way there i  mean  what do you read into all that i mean  what do you think is happening  um well i think that the  the fact that we are being told uh that  charles is on his way that william is on  his way i think that  the palace never wants to create a sense  of anxiety around the royal family what  they try to do is downplay everything  but i think the fact that they are now  saying uh that the uh  next king  and his successor are on the way uh to  balmoral suggests that we are now  entering the last phase of the queen's  life and we don't know how long that  will be and  many will hope that she will recover but  i think that  she has served her country for nearly  100 years  and there must come a time when her life  comes to an end  indeed uh  if it is indeed does come to that it's a  very very sad moment uh  talk us through the procedure what  exactly happens  if the queen does pass away  well there's a highly elaborate uh  set of instructions  it's called operation london bridge and  what it will do is over the next few  days it if the queen should die  there is a highly regimented and well  organized uh  state arrangement  which will take us through her lying in  state  um  her funeral uh her memorial service uh  and the various uh uh public occasions  which will uh close around the end of  her reign um it's all been organized for  a very long time she's been aware of all  the things that are going to happen she  will have put her name to all this so uh  there will be no surprises for those  around the royal family as to what's  going to happen next it's all being set  in train  okay uh christopher wilson royal  biographer really good to get your  thoughts uh joining us there from devon  in the united kingdom
 

